<com:TContent ID="body" >
<h1>Command Line Tool</h1>

<p class="block-content">The optional <tt>prado-cli.php</tt> PHP script file in the <tt>bin</tt>
directory provides command line tools to perform various tedious takes in Prado.
The <tt>prado-cli.php</tt> can be used to create Prado project skeletons, create
initial test fixtures, and access to an interactive PHP shell.
</p>
<h2>Requirements</h2>
<p class="block-content">
To use the command line tool, you need to use your command prompt, command console
or terminal. In addition, PHP must be able to execute PHP scripts from
the command line.
</p>

<h2>Usage</h2>
<p class="block-content">
Inside you application's protected directory, If you type <tt>./vendor/bin/prado-cli</tt>,
you should see the following information.</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
  Command line tools for Prado <com:CurrentVersion />.
usage: php prado-cli.php command[/action] <parameter> [optional]

example: prado-cli http
example: prado-cli help
example: prado-cli cron/tasks

The following options are available:
  -d=<folder>        Loads the configuration.xml/php from <folder>
 --quiet=<level>     Quiets the output to <level> [1..3], default 1 (when specified)
  -q=<level>         is an alias for --quiet

The following commands are available:
  help                         Provides help information about shell commands.
     help/index                Displays available commands or detailed command information.

  shell                        Provides PHP Interactive Shell Interpreter.
     shell/index               Runs a PHP interactive interpreter after Initializing the Prado application.

  http                         Provides a Test PHP Web Server to serve the application.
     http/serve                Runs a PHP Web Server after Initializing the Application.

To see the help of each command, enter:

  prado-cli help <command-name>
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">The <b>&lt;parameter&gt;</b> are required parameters and <b>[optional]</b>
are optional parameters. </p>
<p class="block-content">
A module adds its own commands when the application loads it. The cache modules add
<tt>cache/flush-all</tt>, and the Active Record configuration adds
<tt>activerecord/generate</tt>. The cron, permissions, and database parameter modules add
the commands below. Run <tt>prado-cli help</tt> in the application to list the commands it
has, and <tt>prado-cli help &lt;command&gt;</tt> for one command.
</p>

<h3>Cron commands</h3>
<p class="block-content">
The <a href="?page=Advanced.Cron">cron</a> module (<tt>TCronModule</tt>) adds the
<tt>cron</tt> command to run and inspect the scheduled tasks.</p>
<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli cron</tt></td><td>Displays the registered cron task information. The same as <tt>cron/index</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli cron/run</tt></td><td>Runs the cron tasks that are due. The system cron runs this once a minute.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli cron/tasks</tt></td><td>Displays the cron tasks that the application configures.</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="block-content">
When the module is a <tt>TDbCronManager</tt>, the <tt>cron</tt> command also manages the
tasks stored in the database.</p>
<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli cron/add &lt;task-name&gt; &lt;task-id&gt; &lt;schedule&gt; [parameters...]</tt></td><td>Adds a cron task to the database.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli cron/update &lt;task-name&gt; [parameters...]</tt></td><td>Updates a cron task in the database.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli cron/remove &lt;task-name&gt;</tt></td><td>Removes a cron task from the database.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Permissions commands</h3>
<p class="block-content">
The <a href="?page=Advanced.Permissions">permissions</a> manager
(<tt>TPermissionsManager</tt>) adds the <tt>perm</tt> command to read and change the roles
and rules stored in the database.</p>
<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli perm</tt></td><td>Displays the database permission information. Add <tt>-a</tt> for all. The same as <tt>perm/index</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli perm/role &lt;role-name&gt; [[+-]child ...]</tt></td><td>Adds and removes children of a role in the database. A child with a <tt>+</tt> is added, and a child with a <tt>-</tt> is removed.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli perm/add-rule &lt;permission-name&gt; &lt;action&gt; [users] [roles] [verb] [ips] [priority] [class]</tt></td><td>Adds a rule for a permission to the database.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli perm/remove-rule &lt;permission-name&gt; &lt;db-rule-index&gt;</tt></td><td>Removes a rule for a permission from the database.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Database parameter commands</h3>
<p class="block-content">
The <a href="?page=Configurations.Parameters">database parameter</a> module
(<tt>TDbParameterModule</tt>) adds the <tt>param</tt> command to read and write the
parameters in the database.</p>
<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli param</tt></td><td>Displays all the parameters in the database. The same as <tt>param/index</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli param/get &lt;param-key&gt;</tt></td><td>Gets a parameter by its key.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>prado-cli param/set &lt;param-key&gt; &lt;param-value&gt;</tt></td><td>Sets a parameter to a value.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Requiring a login for commands</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.2.0" />
<p class="block-content">
A command runs without a login by default. <tt>TShellLoginBehavior</tt> requires a username
and password before any command runs. It is a <a href="?page=Configurations.BehaviorsConfig">behavior</a>
that attaches to the <tt>TAuthManager</tt> module, so a command has an authenticated
application user, the same way a page request does.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
Attach the behavior to the <tt>auth</tt> module in the application configuration.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;behavior name="shellLoginAuth" Class="Prado\Shell\TShellLoginBehavior" AttachTo="module:auth" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
With the behavior attached, a command asks for a username and password. The
<tt>--user</tt> option, aliased <tt>-u</tt>, and the <tt>--password</tt> option, aliased
<tt>-p</tt>, supply them on the command line.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
prado-cli --user=admin --password=secret cron/run
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
When the options are absent, the command prompts for the username and the password, and it
hides the password while it is typed. The behavior calls the <tt>login</tt> method of
<tt>TAuthManager</tt> to validate the credentials against the user manager, which the
<a href="?page=Advanced.Auth">Authentication and Authorization</a> page describes. A failed
login stops the command.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
The commands a user may run depend on the user's roles. The
<a href="?page=Advanced.Permissions">permissions</a> manager grants a role the permission
for a command, such as the cron or the permissions commands, so different users run
different commands.
</p>

<h2>Built-in web server</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>http/serve</tt> command runs the application with PHP's built-in web server. It
initializes the application and serves it, so the application runs without a separate web
server. It suits development and testing.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
prado-cli http/serve
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The server listens on <tt>127.0.0.1</tt> and port <tt>8080</tt> by default, so the
application is at <tt>http://127.0.0.1:8080/</tt>. The <tt>--address</tt> option sets the
address, with an optional port, and the <tt>--port</tt> option sets the port. The
<tt>--all</tt> option serves on every network address, and the <tt>--ipv6</tt> option uses
IPv6.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
prado-cli http/serve --address=localhost --port=8777
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Commands from modules and extensions</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The commands listed above are what the tool offers on its own. A module adds more by
registering an action class on the shell application, so the available commands depend on
what the application has configured. The cron, permissions and database parameter commands
all arrive this way, which is why they appear only when those modules are loaded.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
An <a href="?page=Advanced.Plugins">extension</a> registers its commands the same way, and
that page shows the registration.
</p>

<h2>Interactive Shell</h2>
<p class="block-content">
The interactive shell allows you to evaluate PHP statements from the command line.
The <tt>prado-cli.php</tt> script can be used to start the shell and load an existing
Prado project. For example, let us load the blog demo project. Assume that your
command line is in the <tt>prado</tt> distribution directory and you type.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
$: php bin/prado-cli.php shell demos/blog
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The output should be
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
  Command line tools for Prado <com:CurrentVersion />.
Psy Shell v0.11.2 (PHP 7.4.28 — cli) by Justin Hileman
>>>
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Then we will get an instance of the Prado blog application, and from
that instance we want an instance of the <tt>'data'</tt> module.

</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
>> $app = Prado::getApplication();

>> $db = $app->getModule('data');
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Lastly, we want to use the data module to query for a post with <tt>ID=1</tt>. Notice that
we <b>leave out the semicolon</b> to show the results.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
>> $db->queryPostByID(1)
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
There should not be any errors and you should see the following.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content cli">
PostRecord#1
(
    [ID] => 1
    [AuthorID] => 1
    [AuthorName] => 'Prado User'
    [CreateTime] => 1148819691
    [ModifyTime] => 0
    [Title] => 'Welcome to Prado Weblog'
    [Content] => 'Congratulations! You have successfully installed Prado Blog --
 a PRADO-driven weblog system. A default administrator account has been created.
 Please login with <b>admin/prado</b> and update your password as soon as possible.'
    [Status] => 0
    [CommentCount] => 0
)
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Creating Active Record Classes</h2>
<p class="block-content">
In the blog demo project, we need to create two <a href="?page=Database.ActiveRecord">Active Record</a> classes, <tt>UserRecord</tt> and <tt>PostRecord</tt>, to represent data records in the <tt>users</tt> and <tt>posts</tt> tables, respectively. Active Record classes must extend from the base class <tt>ActiveRecord</tt>, and must define property names that matches with the field names of the corresponding table.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
To better organize our directories, we create a new directory <tt>protected/database</tt> to hold the class files. We also modify our application configuration by inserting the following lines. It is equivalent to adding the directory <tt>protected/database</tt> to PHP include_path, which allows us to use the classes without explicitly including them.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content" Language="xml">
<paths>
  <using namespace="Application.database.*" />
</paths>
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
At the prompt, enter the following two commands to create <tt>UserRecord</tt> and <tt>PostRecord</tt> classes:
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source cli" Language="text">
>> generate users Application.database.UserRecord

>> generate posts Application.database.PostRecord
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
Here we used the <a href="?page=Fundamentals.Components1">namespace format</a> again to specify the classes to be created. The path <tt>Application.database.UserRecord</tt> indicates that we want the <tt>UserRecord</tt> class file to be <tt>protected/database/UserRecord.php</tt>.
</p>

</com:TContent>
